{"headline": "Missile attack on Riyadh airport kills 13 and injures hundreds", "pages": 
[
  {
    "text": "A missile attack on King Khalid International Airport in Riyadh, Saudi Arabia, on October 10, 2026, killed 13 people and injured more than 300, according to reports from aviation authorities and the Indian embassy in Saudi Arabia. The Houthis, an Iran-backed group in Yemen, claimed responsibility for the strike, which hit a passenger terminal and caused a fire. The death toll rose to 13 after an Indian national died from injuries sustained in the attack. Other fatalities included four Saudi citizens, a US citizen, and several other foreign nationals. Saudi aviation authorities initially reported 12 deaths and 309 injuries, noting that some victims were in critical condition. A separate projectile reportedly fell near King Fahd International Airport in Dammam on October 10, 2026. The Saudi-led coalition blamed the Houthis for the attack, which it described as the deadliest on Saudi soil in more than a decade, and vowed a response. The Houthis issued a warning to airlines, airport workers, and travelers against using Saudi airports, stating the facilities remain vulnerable to attack."
  },
  {
    "text": "Following the attack, King Khalid International Airport remained closed with no confirmed time for the resumption of passenger flights. Aviation sources indicated that radar and communications equipment were damaged and runways required assessment. Several airlines, including Etihad Airways, Emirates, Flydubai, Lufthansa Group, and SriLankan Airlines, suspended or cancelled flights to Riyadh and other Saudi cities. On October 9, 2026, the European Union Aviation Safety Agency widened its warning to cover more Saudi airspace due to risks from missile and drone attacks, air defense activity, and falling debris. In Riyadh, schools switched to remote learning for the week following instructions from the education ministry. In response to the strike, US President Donald Trump stated that Washington might join Saudi strikes against the Houthis, saying, “We may. We’re going to look at it,” though he did not announce a formal decision. European Commission President Ursula von der Leyen and UN Secretary-General António Guterres both condemned the attack, with Guterres stating that attacks on civilian infrastructure violate international law."
  }
]
}

{
  "headline": "Trump announces partial energy ceasefire between Russia and Ukraine"
,
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "U.S. President Donald Trump announced via Truth Social that Russia and Ukraine have agreed to a partial ceasefire to halt attacks on each other's energy infrastructure. While Trump stated on Sunday that the truce would be effective immediately, reports indicate uncertainty regarding whether either Moscow or Kyiv were aware of the plan. NPR reporter Charles Maynes noted that neither country seemed to have known about the announcement at the time it was made. Following the announcement, Ukrainian President Volodymyr Zelensky stated that Russia is ignoring the declared energy ceasefire and continues to strike Ukrainian facilities. Zelensky reported that Russia hit gas stations, energy infrastructure, and other facilities, resulting in 33 injuries. He suggested the strikes were proof that Russia is unwilling to accept the ceasefire, characterizing the actions as a response from Vladimir Putin to President Trump."
    },
    {
      "text": "On Monday, Russian strikes targeted six Ukrainian energy facilities. According to Ukraine's state news agency Ukrinform, a strike destroyed power grids in Izyum, causing blackouts, while energy and railway infrastructure in western Ukraine was also targeted overnight. Ukraine's state-owned grid operator, Ukrenergo, reported new power cuts in Kharkiv, Donetsk, and Zaporizhzhia due to damage to energy equipment, leading to electricity rationing in parts of the country. Additionally, emergency services responded to a Russian drone strike on a residential building in Kharkiv. Separately, Russian Foreign Minister Sergei Lavrov stated on Monday that Ukraine does not deserve the respite a ceasefire would provide."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "Kwesimintsim MP reports single Legal Aid lawyer for Tamale and Bolgatanga, Ghana",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "Philip Fiifi Buckman, the Member of Parliament for Kwesimintsim and a member of Parliament’s Constitutional and Legal Affairs Committee, described Legal Aid Commission services in northern Ghana as “pathetic” during the launch of the 2026 Legal Aid Week in Accra. Recounting a recent oversight visit to the northern regions, Mr. Buckman stated that Tamale and Bolgatanga had only one lawyer serving the Commission between them. He noted that the committee was informed the same lawyer was responsible for both cities, which he characterized as a serious shortage of personnel for those unable to afford legal representation. Mr. Buckman urged the government to take decisive steps to resource the Commission so it can fulfill its mandate under the Legal Aid Commission Act, 2018 (Act 977), which requires the agency to provide legal assistance to indigent and vulnerable people."
    },
    {
      "text": "The report follows an appeal by the Legal Aid Commission’s Board Chairman, Supreme Court Justice Samuel Kwame Asiedu Adibu, for urgent support to address office and staffing shortages. Justice Adibu stated that the Commission currently has 190 staff nationwide and operates 45 offices across Ghana’s 261 districts, maintaining a presence in only 10 of the country’s 16 regions. He disclosed that the Commission is short by approximately 180 staff, a deficit he said limits the ability to reach individuals in need of legal services."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "Bangladesh Election Commission plans union parishad polls in five phases starting January",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "The Election Commission in Bangladesh is planning to conduct union parishad elections in five phases, potentially beginning in the third week of January. On October 12, 2026, Election Commissioner Abul Fazal Md Sanaullah stated that the polls are intended to be held on January 21, February 4, April 29, May 13, and May 27. All five proposed polling dates fall on Thursdays. The commission noted that the number of phases was reduced from seven to five due to time constraints. The Election Commission plans to hold the polls simultaneously across all districts, prioritizing union parishads and Sadar upazilas where terms have already expired."
    },
    {
      "text": "The revised timeline follows previous scheduling discussions. On September 14, the Election Commission had announced November 12 as a tentative election date. However, a cabinet member expressed reservations, stating the date was announced without government consultation. Following an inter-ministerial meeting on September 17, top government officials suggested conducting the polls from January. Election Commissioner Sanaullah confirmed the commission met with representatives from all government departments on September 17, stating that election dates would be announced following the completion of desk work. The commission expects to announce election schedules approximately six weeks before each polling date, with a range of 38 to 45 days."
    }
  ]
}

